  "title": "The Officers at Newburgh",
  "subtitle": "George Washington, the Newburgh Conspiracy, and the Crisis That Forged the American Republic",
  "authorDisplayName": "Liszt Think",
  "description": "In March 1783, peace had been signed but the Continental Army remained unpaid and restless at Newburgh. Two anonymous addresses circulated through camp, urging officers to defy Congress and march on Philadelphia. Nationalists in the capital secretly hoped this mutiny would force federal taxing power, risking military rule to secure financial stability. George Washington intervened uninvited at a tense assembly, reading a letter and donning spectacles to plead for patience. Drawing on diaries, congressional debates, and private correspondence, this narrative reconstructs the conspiracy from the documents themselves. It traces the plot from John Armstrong Jr.’s pen to Horatio Gates’s silence, revealing how a fragile republic survived its most dangerous internal threat. The crisis did not end with Washington’s emotional appeal; its unresolved debts echoed through Shays’s Rebellion and shaped the Constitution’s fiscal architecture. This is the definitive account of how an army’s grievance became a nation’s founding test.",
